                                                <div class="person-dialog__content">
                                                    <div class="person-dialog__header margin-trim">
                                                        <h2>Mischa Jardine</h2>

                                                                                                                    <h4>Janina</h4>
                                                                                                            </div>

                                                    <div class="person-dialog__bio">
                                                        <p class="type-body-xl"></p>

                                                        <div class="person-dialog__bio__main__text">
                                                            <p>Training: Bristol Old Vic Theatre School (MA Acting) and Royal Welsh College of Music and Drama (BMus Music)</p>
<p>Theatre Credits include: RAPUNZEL (Tobacco Factory); OLION (Frân Wen Theatre Company); PIED PIPER OF MARYLEBONE (Regent&#8217;s Park Festival); TWO SISTERS (Union Theatre); THE LION, THE WITCH AND THE WARDROBE (Birmingham Rep); THE CARE TAKERS (Wise Children); and 101 DALMATIANS (Regent&#8217;s Park Open Air Theatre).</p>
<p>Mischa is a classically trained singer and cellist. She recently participated in R&amp;Ds for two theatre companies, and will be R&amp;Ding her own play, Based on the Life of Jacqueline Du Pré, to be presented at the Wales Millennium Centre. Additionally, Mischa was Assistant Composer for the film The Quackening and played cello on the radio production of Stoma for BBC Radio. She also enjoys collaborating with jazz, pop, and folk artists, and continues to develop her own compositions.</p>

                                                        </div>
                                                    </div>
                                                </div>
                                            </article>
                                        </template>
                                    
                                    


